A click, a key press, typing into a box... Everything that happens on a page is an **event**. `addEventListener` tells an element "when this event happens, run this function":

```js
const btn = document.querySelector("#start");
btn.addEventListener("click", () => {
  console.log("Clicked!");
});
```

The function doesn't run right away; it runs **every time** the event happens. Open the page and click the button yourself to try it.

Careful: when you pass a named function, don't add parentheses. `btn.addEventListener("click", launch)` is right; if you write `launch()`, the function runs once immediately and doesn't listen for clicks.

The browser gives the listener function an **event object**, usually called `e`. Its most used property is `e.target`: the element where the event happened.

```js
const buttons = document.querySelectorAll(".planet-btn");
for (const b of buttons) {
  b.addEventListener("click", (e) => {
    console.log("Chosen:", e.target.textContent);
  });
}
```

`e.type` gives the event's name (`"click"`). You can attach the same function to many buttons and find out which one was clicked with `e.target`.

Every time a letter is typed into an `<input>` box, an `input` event happens. The text in the box is in the `value` property:

```js
const nameBox = document.querySelector("#name");
nameBox.addEventListener("input", () => {
  document.querySelector("#hello").textContent = "Hello, " + nameBox.value;
});
```

On interactive pages we keep data in a variable; this is called **state**. An event changes the state, then we update the screen from the state:

```js
let lives = 3;
hitBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
  lives--;                                   // 1) change the state
  livesText.textContent = "Lives: " + lives; // 2) update the screen
});
```

To remove a listener, call `removeEventListener` with the same event name and **the same function**; that's why we give the function a name:

```js
function onLaunch() {
  console.log("Launch!");
  btn.removeEventListener("click", onLaunch); // won't run again
}
btn.addEventListener("click", onLaunch);
```

The shortcut: `btn.addEventListener("click", onLaunch, { once: true })`.

**Event delegation**: a click event starts at the clicked element and **bubbles up** to the elements around it. So instead of adding a separate listener to every button, you can add **one** listener to the element that contains them:

```js
const menu = document.querySelector("#menu");
menu.addEventListener("click", (e) => {
  if (e.target.tagName !== "BUTTON") return; // ignore it if it's not a button
  console.log("Route:", e.target.textContent);
});
```

The nice part: buttons **added to the menu later** work too. That will be really useful tomorrow when you create elements with code.

examples.code:
let count = 0;
const btn = document.querySelector("#btn");

btn.addEventListener("click", (e) => {
  count++;
  document.querySelector("#info").textContent = `${count} clicks`;
  console.log("Clicked:", e.target.textContent);
});

const input = document.querySelector("#name");
const hello = document.querySelector("#hello");

input.addEventListener("input", () => {
  hello.textContent = "Hello, " + input.value + "!";
});

const colors = { red: "#d1242f", blue: "#1f6feb", green: "#1a7f37" };
document.querySelector("#colors").addEventListener("click", (e) => {
  if (e.target.tagName !== "BUTTON") return;
  hello.style.color = colors[e.target.textContent];
});

